{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NfoE71mpCZD6vzVTm6Ro93","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tid: srd021-tac\n2\ttitle: \"cmd/tac: Concatenate and Print Files in Reverse\"\n3\t\n4\tproblem: |\n5\t  tac is the reverse of cat: it prints lines of its input in reverse order. macOS does not\n6\t  ship a native tac equivalent; scripts that use tac depend on the Homebrew GNU binary\n7\t  (gtac, installed by coreutils). The Go implementation must match gtac byte-for-byte on\n8\t  all flag and input combinations, including custom separators and binary input, verified\n9\t  by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n10\t\n11\tgoals:\n12\t  - G1: Read each file (or stdin) and write its records in reverse order, matching GNU tac behavior.\n13\t  - G2: Support custom record separators via -s, enabling reversal on boundaries other than newlines.\n14\t  - G3: Support -b to position the separator before the record rather than after it.\n15\t  - G4: Support -r to interpret the separator as a regular expression.\n16\t\n17\trequirements:\n18\t  R1:\n19\t    title: Core reversal behavior\n20\t    items:\n21\t      - R1.1:\n22\t          text: Must read the entire input into memory, split it into records on the separator (default newline), and write the records in reverse order to stdout.\n23\t      - R1.2:\n24\t          text: Must treat a trailing separator at the end of input as the terminator of the last record, not as a separator before an empty record. This matches gtac behavior where \"a\\nb\\n\" reversed produces \"b\\na\\n\" not \"\\nb\\na\".\n25\t      - R1.3:\n26\t          text: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given, or when \"-\" appears as a filename.\n27\t      - R1.4:\n28\t          text: When multiple files are given, must process each file independently and write them in argument order (each file's records reversed, files not merged before reversal).\n29\t\n30\t  R2:\n31\t    title: Separator options (-s, -b, -r)\n32\t    items:\n33\t      - R2.1:\n34\t          text: -s SEP must use SEP as the record separator instead of newline. SEP may be any string.\n35\t      - R2.2:\n36\t          text: By default the separator follows each record. -b must place the separator before each record instead of after it.\n37\t      - R2.3:\n38\t          text: -r must interpret the -s SEP argument as a regular expression (Go regexp syntax). Each match of the pattern becomes a record boundary.\n39\t      - R2.4:\n40\t          text: When -s and -r are combined, the separator is a regex; records are the text between consecutive matches.\n41\t\n42\t  R3:\n43\t    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n44\t    items:\n45\t      - R3.1:\n46\t          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n47\t      - R3.2:\n48\t          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened or read, printing the error to stderr. Processing continues for remaining files.\n49\t      - R3.3:\n50\t          text: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n51\t      - R3.4:\n52\t          text: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n53\t\n54\t  R4:\n55\t    title: Differential testing\n56\t    items:\n57\t      - R4.1:\n58\t          text: Differential tests must compare Go tac output against gtac byte-for-byte via pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests.\n59\t      - R4.2:\n60\t          text: \"Tests must cover: single-file reversal, stdin reversal, multi-file reversal, -s with a custom separator, -b flag, and a file with no trailing newline.\"\n61\t      - R4.3:\n62\t          text: Tests must use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent divergence.\n63\t\n64\tnon_goals:\n65\t  - cmd/tac does not implement the block-level backward seek optimization from the C source; it buffers the full input in memory.\n66\t  - cmd/tac does not support files too large to fit in memory; it accumulates all input before producing output.\n67\t\n68\tacceptance_criteria:\n69\t  - id: AC1\n70\t    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' | tac produces 'c\\\\nb\\\\na\\\\n', matching gtac byte-for-byte.\"\n71\t    traces:\n72\t      - R1.1\n73\t      - R1.2\n74\t      - R1.3\n75\t      - R1.4\n76\t  - id: AC2\n77\t    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc' | tac produces 'c\\\\nb\\\\na' (no trailing newline), matching gtac.\"\n78\t    traces:\n79\t      - R1.1\n80\t      - R1.2\n81\t      - R1.3\n82\t      - R1.4\n83\t  - id: AC3\n84\t    criterion: \"tac -s : on 'a:b:c:' produces 'c:b:a:', matching gtac -s :.\"\n85\t    traces:\n86\t      - R2.1\n87\t      - R2.3\n88\t      - R2.4\n89\t      - R4.2\n90\t  - id: AC4\n91\t    criterion: \"tac -b -s : on ':a:b:c' produces ':c:b:a', matching gtac -b -s :.\"\n92\t    traces:\n93\t      - R2.1\n94\t      - R2.2\n95\t      - R2.3\n96\t      - R2.4\n97\t      - R4.2\n98\t  - id: AC5\n99\t    criterion: \"Differential test against gtac passes for all required test cases.\"\n100\t    traces:\n101\t      - R4.1\n102\t  - id: AC6\n103\t    criterion: \"cmd/tac compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n104\t    traces:\n105\t      - R1.1\n106\t      - R1.2\n107\t      - R1.3\n108\t      - R1.4\n109\t      - R2.1\n110\t      - R2.2\n111\t      - R2.3\n112\t      - R2.4\n113\t      - R3.1\n114\t      - R3.2\n115\t      - R3.3\n116\t      - R3.4\n117\t      - R4.1\n118\t      - R4.2\n119\t      - R4.3\n120\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fdf0d090-c249-41e5-bb5e-92f586103683","uuid":"73e64d8f-b9da-40e2-aa14-25a11162c1f2","timestamp":"2026-05-15T03:26:22.246Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5256/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd021-tac.yaml","content":"id: srd021-tac\ntitle: \"cmd/tac: Concatenate and Print Files in Reverse\"\n\nproblem: |\n  tac is the reverse of cat: it prints lines of its input in reverse order. macOS does not\n  ship a native tac equivalent; scripts that use tac depend on the Homebrew GNU binary\n  (gtac, installed by coreutils). The Go implementation must match gtac byte-for-byte on\n  all flag and input combinations, including custom separators and binary input, verified\n  by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Read each file (or stdin) and write its records in reverse order, matching GNU tac behavior.\n  - G2: Support custom record separators via -s, enabling reversal on boundaries other than newlines.\n  - G3: Support -b to position the separator before the record rather than after it.\n  - G4: Support -r to interpret the separator as a regular expression.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Core reversal behavior\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: Must read the entire input into memory, split it into records on the separator (default newline), and write the records in reverse order to stdout.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: Must treat a trailing separator at the end of input as the terminator of the last record, not as a separator before an empty record. This matches gtac behavior where \"a\\nb\\n\" reversed produces \"b\\na\\n\" not \"\\nb\\na\".\n      - R1.3:\n          text: Must read from stdin when no file arguments are given, or when \"-\" appears as a filename.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: When multiple files are given, must process each file independently and write them in argument order (each file's records reversed, files not merged before reversal).\n\n  R2:\n    title: Separator options (-s, -b, -r)\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: -s SEP must use SEP as the record separator instead of newline. SEP may be any string.\n      - R2.2:\n          text: By default the separator follows each record. -b must place the separator before each record instead of after it.\n      - R2.3:\n          text: -r must interpret the -s SEP argument as a regular expression (Go regexp syntax). Each match of the pattern becomes a record boundary.\n      - R2.4:\n          text: When -s and -r are combined, the separator is a regex; records are the text between consecutive matches.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened or read, printing the error to stderr. Processing continues for remaining files.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n      - R3.4:\n          text: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Differential testing\n    items:\n      - R4.1:\n          text: Differential tests must compare Go tac output against gtac byte-for-byte via pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests.\n      - R4.2:\n          text: \"Tests must cover: single-file reversal, stdin reversal, multi-file reversal, -s with a custom separator, -b flag, and a file with no trailing newline.\"\n      - R4.3:\n          text: Tests must use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent divergence.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/tac does not implement the block-level backward seek optimization from the C source; it buffers the full input in memory.\n  - cmd/tac does not support files too large to fit in memory; it accumulates all input before producing output.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - id: AC1\n    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n' | tac produces 'c\\\\nb\\\\na\\\\n', matching gtac byte-for-byte.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC2\n    criterion: \"printf 'a\\\\nb\\\\nc' | tac produces 'c\\\\nb\\\\na' (no trailing newline), matching gtac.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n  - id: AC3\n    criterion: \"tac -s : on 'a:b:c:' produces 'c:b:a:', matching gtac -s :.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n      - R4.2\n  - id: AC4\n    criterion: \"tac -b -s : on ':a:b:c' produces ':c:b:a', matching gtac -b -s :.\"\n    traces:\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n      - R4.2\n  - id: AC5\n    criterion: \"Differential test against gtac passes for all required test cases.\"\n    traces:\n      - R4.1\n  - id: AC6\n    criterion: \"cmd/tac compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    traces:\n      - R1.1\n      - R1.2\n      - R1.3\n      - R1.4\n      - R2.1\n      - R2.2\n      - R2.3\n      - R2.4\n      - R3.1\n      - R3.2\n      - R3.3\n      - R3.4\n      - R4.1\n      - R4.2\n      - R4.3\n","numLines":120,"startLine":1,"totalLines":120}}}
